---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { SECTIONS } from "../data/sections";

const title = "Best Walking Pads With Handrail (2026): 2-in-1 Folding Picks";
const description =
  "Five walking pads with a fold-up rail or side handles, from a $100 2-in-1 to a 47-inch premium deck, picked from published specs and independent coverage. What the rail really does.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline",
    name: "Sunny Health & Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline",
    brand: "Sunny Health & Fitness",
    category: "Best Overall Walking Pad With Handrail",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0BM2SRJ9D?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/417uQ6wp8XL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "About $487 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":487,"high":487},
    specs: "45.7 x 16 in. running surface · 0.5–3.7 mph handlebar down, to 6 mph handlebar up · 7% auto incline · 265 lb capacity (Sunny)",
    pros: [
      "A powered incline to 7% in six preset steps, changed at the press of a button",
      "A 45.7-inch running surface by both the listing and Sunny’s site, second only to the WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+ here",
      "Sunny publishes a weight limit (265 lb), unit weight (79.4 lb) and deck height on its own store",
      "A US-based brand with its own support and the SunnyFit app",
    ],
    cons: [
      "About $487, more than twice the UREVO Strol 2E Pro",
      "Running mode stops at 6 mph, lower than the Goplus SuperFit",
      "At 79.4 lb by Sunny’s figure, the heaviest pad here to move",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The 2-in-1 to buy for most people: a fold-up rail, a powered incline you can change mid-walk and a 45.7-inch running surface. It is a walking machine first, with a jog in reserve.",
    description: "Sunny's Treadpad with 7% auto incline is the most complete front-rail 2-in-1 we could check on Amazon. The Amazon listing gives a 45.7 by 16-inch running surface, a walking mode of 0.5 to 3.7 mph with the handlebar folded and a running mode up to 6 mph with it fully extended, a powered incline with six preset levels up to 7%, a 6.3 by 2.4-inch display, a remote and the SunnyFit app. Sunny's own store lists the same running surface and speed ranges for its 2-in-1 auto-incline Treadpad and adds a 265 lb maximum user weight, a 79.4 lb product weight, a 2.0 HP peak motor and a deck that sits 4.1 inches off the floor flat and 5.7 inches at full incline, which also makes it a reasonable choice under a low ceiling. Its running surface is about five inches longer than the Goplus SuperFit's, the most meaningful difference for jogging, because it gives a longer stride somewhere to land. The trade-offs are the price and the weight: at nearly 80 lb it is a two-handed job to move. Amazon showed it at $487.26 on 6 November 2026. Sunny's warranty on its own store is three years on the frame and 180 days on other parts.",
  },
  {
    id: "walkingpad-r3-hybrid-plus",
    name: "WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+",
    brand: "WalkingPad",
    category: "Best Premium Walking Pad With Handrails",
    badge: "Best Premium",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F8VSLK43?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31EHF8W4eGL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$800–$1,000",
    priceBounds: {"low":800,"high":1000},
    specs: "47.2 x 17.3 in. walking area · 1–7.5 mph · folding side handrails · 265 lb capacity",
    pros: [
      "A 47.2 by 17.3-inch deck — the longest and widest on this page — which tall walkers and anyone jogging will notice",
      "Side handrails that fold away, giving a hand on each side rather than a single bar in front",
      "Sold through WalkingPad, its specialist retailers and a major US electronics chain",
      "WalkingPad is one of the longest-established walking pad brands, with an optional warranty extension",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Costs far more than the Sunny or UREVO pads here, and has no incline",
      "WalkingPad lists different capacities for different R3 versions on the same page, so confirm you are buying the Hybrid+ and its 265 lb rating",
      "We found no independent hands-on review of the Hybrid+; the standard warranty is one year",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a tall walker, or anyone who wants the most deck a folding 2-in-1 offers, with handles on both sides. Hard to justify on price alone.",
    description: "The R3 Hybrid+ is the premium option, and its case rests on the deck. WalkingPad lists a 47.2 by 17.3-inch walking area, a speed range of 1 to 7.5 mph and a 265 lb capacity, with folding side handrails, and the specialist retailers selling it give the same figures. That deck is about five inches longer than the UREVO’s and nearly an inch wider, which matters for a tall walker and for anyone who wants a jog to feel less cramped. The side handrails put a hand on each side of your body and fold away for under-desk use; one retailer listing says lowering them limits the pad to 4 mph, which is consistent with how most 2-in-1 pads work. The cautions are about clarity rather than the machine. WalkingPad’s R3 page lists several versions with different capacities, including a 330 lb flat pad and 265 lb Hybrid models, so check the version in your basket. We found no independent hands-on review. And the price — $799 on sale from $999 when we checked — buys deck size, not extra features: there is no incline.",
  },
  {
    id: "goplus-superfit-2-in-1",
    name: "Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1",
    brand: "Goplus",
    category: "Best Budget Walking Pad With Handrail",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B07ZRSBP5S?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41jxCgjZICL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: "/goplus-2-in-1-treadmill/",
    priceRange: "$100–$200",
    priceBounds: {"low":100,"high":200},
    specs: "40 x 16 in. belt · up to 2.5 mph rail down, 7.5 mph rail up · 265 lb capacity · about 5 in. folded",
    pros: [
      "One of the cheapest established 2-in-1 pads, frequently on sale between about $100 and $200",
      "A hands-on review confirmed the rail locks upright and unlocks a 7.5 mph running mode",
      "Folds to about 5 inches tall, slim enough for most sofas and beds",
      "Quick to set up — the hands-on reviewer took about 20 to 25 minutes",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Walking mode is capped at about 2.5 mph with the rail down, which is slow for brisk walkers at a desk",
      "The display sits low on the deck and is hard to read while walking, and the infrared remote needs a line of sight",
      "The handles are bolted on and have to come off before the rail folds, and the 40 by 16-inch belt is short for running",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a light, occasional user on a tight budget who walks slowly and wants a rail to hold. Brisk walkers should spend more.",
    description: "The SuperFit is the long-running budget 2-in-1, and unlike most pads at this price it has been through a published hands-on review. Retail listings and that review agree on a belt of about 40 by 16 inches, a 265 lb capacity and a folded height of about 5 inches, and the review confirmed the defining detail: with the rail folded down the pad is capped at about 2.5 mph, and once the rail is locked upright a running mode up to 7.5 mph becomes available. That makes it an honest example of the rail as a speed switch. The 2.5 mph cap is the main thing to understand before buying. It is a comfortable typing pace, but brisk walkers will want to raise the rail, which takes the pad out from under the desk. The reviewer also found the display hard to read because it sits low on the deck, the remote awkward under a desk, and the handles bolted on in a way that has to be undone to fold the rail. The belt is short and narrow for running. At the $100 to $200 it has commonly sold for, those compromises are fair.",
  },
  {
    id: "urevo-strol-2e-pro",
    name: "UREVO Strol 2E Pro",
    brand: "UREVO",
    category: "Best Budget 2-in-1 With Incline",
    badge: "Lightest",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H1H4JT4C?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/410e3-vj4nL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$198–$220 (Amazon, 6 Nov 2026)",
    priceBounds: {"low":198,"high":220},
    specs: "44 lb (listing) · 0.6–4 mph walking mode · up to 6.2 mph running mode · 12% manual incline · tool-free fold",
    pros: [
      "A 12% manual incline, which makes a slow walk much harder without needing more speed",
      "At 44 lb by its listing, by far the lightest pad here",
      "A 0.6–4 mph walking mode and a 6.2 mph running mode with the handle up",
      "App with virtual routes and an auto-speed mode, if you like that sort of thing",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A manual incline means stepping off to change it, unlike the Sunny’s powered incline",
      "The bullets give no belt size or weight limit on either listing we checked",
      "The listing copy leans on marketing claims (\"full sprints\") that a 6.2 mph pad cannot back up",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The cheapest 2-in-1 here with a real incline, and the lightest by its stated weight. Set the incline before you start and treat the running mode as a jog.",
    description: "The Strol 2E Pro is the Strol 2E with an incline and a lighter frame. Its Amazon listings describe a walking mode of 0.6 to 4 mph and a running mode of 0.4 to 6.2 mph, a 12% manual incline, a fold that needs no tools and glide wheels, and a weight of 44 lb, which the title and bullets both state. Incline is the most useful feature a walking machine can add once speed stops being comfortable: a few percent at 3 mph raises the effort noticeably without lengthening your stride, which suits the short belts on 2-in-1s better than more speed does. Because the incline is manual, you set it before you start rather than changing it mid-walk. The app adds virtual routes and an automatic speed mode that follows your pace, which some people like and others switch off. What the listing does not give in its bullets is the belt size or a maximum user weight, so confirm both with UREVO if you are tall or heavy. Two listings were live on 6 November 2026, at $197.98 and $219.99, with matching descriptions; check that the one in your basket is the Pro with the incline, not the plain Strol 2E.",
  },
  {
    id: "walkingpad-mx8",
    name: "WalkingPad MX8",
    brand: "WalkingPad",
    category: "Best Walking Pad With Side Handles",
    badge: "Best Side Handles",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GR5R4QXX?tag=sktiger-20&linkCode=ll1",
    amazonLabel: "Check Price on Amazon",
    image: {"url":"https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/318wExIbelL._SL500_.jpg","width":500,"height":500},
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$550–$700",
    priceBounds: {"low":550,"high":700},
    specs: "330 lb capacity · 39.4 x 15 in. running area · 1–5 mph · removable side handles",
    pros: [
      "Handles on both sides, so you can steady yourself with either hand without reaching forward",
      "The handles detach for under-desk use and are described as height-adjustable",
      "A 330 lb capacity stated by WalkingPad and two specialist retailers — the highest on this page",
      "The deck folds in half and stands upright in about 18 by 26 inches of floor",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 15-inch-wide running area is narrow, and a 5 mph top speed is a brisk walk rather than a jog",
      "We found no independent hands-on review, and one specialist retailer listed it as a pre-order",
      "Only a 2.25 HP motor figure is published, and the warranty is one year",
    ],
    bottomLine: "For a walker who wants a steadying hand on each side and a machine that stores in a corner. Not for anyone who wants to jog.",
    description: "The MX8 takes a different approach to the rail. Instead of a single bar in front, it has a removable handle on each side, which WalkingPad describes as adjustable for height. For a walker who wants to steady themselves — stepping on, changing speed, or turning to look at a screen — a hand on each side is often more natural than reaching forward, and it keeps your posture upright. WalkingPad’s product page and two specialist retailers agree on a 330 lb capacity, a 39.4 by 15-inch running area and a speed range of 1 to 5 mph. We found no speed limit tied to the handles; the pad simply tops out at a brisk walk, so it is not the choice for jogging. The deck folds in half and stands upright, which suits a small room with a corner to spare. The cautions: the running area is narrow at 15 inches, we found no independent review, one retailer listed it for pre-order, and the warranty is one year. It sold for $549 against a $699 list price when we checked.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Best Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Best Walking Pads With Handrail", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why do some walking pads only go faster with the handrail up?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On most 2-in-1 pads the rail position doubles as a mode switch. With the rail folded the pad is treated as an under-desk walking pad and capped at a walking speed, often 2.5 to 4 mph. Raising and locking the rail unlocks a running mode, typically 6 to 7.6 mph, on the basis that you have something to hold and the safety clip within reach.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is a walking pad with a handrail safer?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It can be, for stepping on and off and for moments when you lose your balance, because you have something to grab. But a pad's rail is a single folding upright or a pair of short handles on a light deck. It steadies you; it is not built to take your weight. Anyone who needs real support should use a treadmill with full-length side rails.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can seniors use a walking pad with a handrail?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some can, for light walking, if they are steady on their feet and use the rail only as an occasional aid. Anyone who needs to hold on throughout, or has a balance concern, is better served by a treadmill with full side rails, a low step-up height and a slow start speed. Check with a doctor or physiotherapist first if balance is a concern.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use a 2-in-1 walking pad under a desk with the rail up?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually not with the desk over the deck, because the rail is in the way. Most people fold the rail down for desk walking and raise it for workouts. If you want a rail while you work, position the desk behind or beside the rail, or choose a model with a desk tray on the rail, such as UREVO's FoldiMix range.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you run on a 2-in-1 walking pad?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A short, easy jog with the rail up is realistic on some, especially those with longer running surfaces, such as the Sunny Treadpad (45.7 inches) or WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+. Regular running is not: the belts are still short, the motors are sized for walking, and the rail is not built for running forces. Use running mode as an occasional extra.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are side handles better than a front handrail?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They suit different needs. Side handles let you steady yourself with either hand without reaching forward, which keeps your posture upright at walking speed. A front rail gives a bar to rest both hands on, often carries the controls or a desk tray, and on most pads is what unlocks running speeds. Side handles are usually shorter and less suited to jogging.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-13",
      dateModified: "2026-10-13",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      ...(product.brand ? { brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.brand } } : {}),
      ...(product.image ? { image: product.image.url } : {}),
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 5,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};

// "More in" links: the other pages in this article's hub group.
const HERE = "/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/";
const HUB = SECTIONS.find((s) => s.slug === "best");
const GROUP = HUB?.groups.find((g) => g.pages.some((p) => p.url === HERE)) ?? HUB?.groups[0];
const MORE = (GROUP?.pages ?? []).filter((p) => p.url !== HERE).slice(0, 6);
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-13",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-13",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "walking pad with handrail",
      "2 in 1 walking pad",
      "folding walking pad with handle",
      "walking pad with handle bar",
      "under desk treadmill with handrail",
      "urevo strol 2s pro",
      "goplus superfit 2 in 1",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Best Overall: Sunny Health & Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline", link: PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/best/">Best Treadmills</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Buying Guide</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Best Walking Pads With Handrail</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        A 2-in-1 walking pad folds flat under a desk, then lifts a rail when you want something to hold — and, on most, a higher top speed. Here are five worth considering, what the rail is really for, and when you need a proper treadmill instead.
      </p>
      <p class="ed-answer"><strong>Short answer.</strong> Best Overall: <a href="#sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline">Sunny Health & Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline</a>. Best Premium: <a href="#walkingpad-r3-hybrid-plus">WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+</a>. Best Budget: <a href="#goplus-superfit-2-in-1">Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1</a>.</p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated October 13, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS
        </div>
        <a href="#top-picks" class="btn-ghost">View products</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        We research every product on this page independently. If you buy through our links, we may earn a
        commission at no cost to you. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Learn more about how we review.</a>
      </p>

      <figure class="ed-hero">
        <div class="ed-hero-collage">
          <a href="#sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/417uQ6wp8XL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#walkingpad-r3-hybrid-plus"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/31EHF8W4eGL._SL500_.jpg" alt="WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
          <a href="#goplus-superfit-2-in-1"><img src="https://m.media-amazon.com/images/I/41jxCgjZICL._SL500_.jpg" alt="Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1" width="500" height="500" decoding="async" /></a>
        </div>
        <figcaption>Sunny Health &amp; Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline · WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+ · Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Most walking pads have nothing to hold. That is fine for slow, steady walking at a desk,
          but plenty of people want a hand on something — when they step on, when the belt speeds
          up, or simply because it feels steadier. A walking pad with a handrail answers that, and
          on most models raising the rail also unlocks a higher top speed for a light jog. This
          page sorts the 2-in-1 pads worth considering from the listings, explains what the rail
          can and cannot do, and says plainly who needs a real treadmill instead.
        </p>

        <section id="top-picks" class="ed-toppicks not-prose">
          <h2>Our top picks:</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Overall:</span> <a href="#sunny-treadpad-7-auto-incline">Sunny Health & Fitness Treadpad Treadmill 7% Auto Incline</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[0].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Premium:</span> <a href="#walkingpad-r3-hybrid-plus">WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[1].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Budget:</span> <a href="#goplus-superfit-2-in-1">Goplus SuperFit 2-in-1</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[2].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Lightest:</span> <a href="#urevo-strol-2e-pro">UREVO Strol 2E Pro</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[3].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
            <li>
              <span class="tp-label"><span class="tp-award">Best Side Handles:</span> <a href="#walkingpad-mx8">WalkingPad MX8</a></span>
              <a href={PRODUCTS[4].amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="buy-pill">Amazon &rarr;</a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <aside class="ed-note not-prose">
          <span class="ed-note-k">What we checked</span>
          <strong class="text-gray-900">On the products below.</strong> We have not tested these
          walking pads. The specifications on this page were checked on 13 October 2026 against
          each brand's own product page (WalkingPad and, for the Sunny, Sunny's store), the Amazon, brand-store or
          specialist-retailer listing, and independent coverage — a published hands-on review for
          the Goplus SuperFit, and a long-running spec tracker that reads
          owner's manuals and motor labels. A figure appears in a product's specification line
          only where those sources agree. Where they did not — most often on weight capacity and
          motor type — we left the figure out and say so in the text. None of these are our own
          measurements. Prices are those we saw on brand and retailer sites on 13 October 2026,
          except the Goplus, where the band reflects recent tracked prices; all of them change
          often, as do specifications under unchanged product names, so confirm the price, the
          specification and the warranty terms against the exact unit before you order. Nothing on
          this page is medical advice. We earn a commission if you buy through the Amazon links on
          this page; it does not change which products we list or what we say about them.
        </aside>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="In this guide">
          <h2>In this guide</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#quick-picks">Our Picks at a Glance</a></li>
            <li><a href="#picks">The 5 Best Walking Pads With a Handrail</a></li>
            <li><a href="#how-we-chose">How We Chose These Walking Pads</a></li>
            <li><a href="#rail-purpose">Balance Aid or Speed Unlock: What the Rail Is For</a></li>
            <li><a href="#rail-stability">How Stable Is a Fold-Up Rail?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#folded-height">Folded Height, Storage and Desk Fit</a></li>
            <li><a href="#who-should-skip">Who Should Buy a Full Treadmill Instead</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="quick-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Our Picks at a Glance</h2>
<p>
            Five pads, covering the main ways a pad can give you something to hold. The short
