        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why do some walking pads only go faster with the handrail up?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On most 2-in-1 pads the rail position doubles as a mode switch. With the rail folded the pad is treated as an under-desk walking pad and capped at a walking speed, often 2.5 to 4 mph. Raising and locking the rail unlocks a running mode, typically 6 to 7.6 mph, on the basis that you have something to hold and the safety clip within reach.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a walking pad with a handrail safer?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It can be, for stepping on and off and for moments when you lose your balance, because you have something to grab. But a pad's rail is a single folding upright or a pair of short handles on a light deck. It steadies you; it is not built to take your weight. Anyone who needs real support should use a treadmill with full-length side rails.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can seniors use a walking pad with a handrail?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Some can, for light walking, if they are steady on their feet and use the rail only as an occasional aid. Anyone who needs to hold on throughout, or has a balance concern, is better served by a treadmill with full side rails, a low step-up height and a slow start speed. Check with a doctor or physiotherapist first if balance is a concern.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you use a 2-in-1 walking pad under a desk with the rail up?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually not with the desk over the deck, because the rail is in the way. Most people fold the rail down for desk walking and raise it for workouts. If you want a rail while you work, position the desk behind or beside the rail, or choose a model with a desk tray on the rail, such as UREVO's FoldiMix range.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a 2-in-1 walking pad?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              A short, easy jog with the rail up is realistic on some, especially those with longer running surfaces, such as the Sunny Treadpad (45.7 inches) or WalkingPad R3 Hybrid+. Regular running is not: the belts are still short, the motors are sized for walking, and the rail is not built for running forces. Use running mode as an occasional extra.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Are side handles better than a front handrail?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              They suit different needs. Side handles let you steady yourself with either hand without reaching forward, which keeps your posture upright at walking speed. A front rail gives a bar to rest both hands on, often carries the controls or a desk tray, and on most pads is what unlocks running speeds. Side handles are usually shorter and less suited to jogging.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
